# Text policy.
#
# Line endings are pinned so a checkout reproduces exactly what was committed,
# regardless of the machine's core.autocrlf: a `core.autocrlf=true` clone would
# otherwise rewrite line endings and make committed files differ from the ones
# the tests and generators produce.
* -text

# These are text; keep them LF everywhere so generated == committed.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.pyw   text eol=lf
*.cmd   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# Windows PowerShell 5.1 -- still the default powershell.exe on Windows 10/11 --
# decodes a BOM-less .ps1 as ANSI, so the Chinese strings in build.ps1 and
# tools/vendor_ffmpeg.ps1 become mojibake and the file fails to *parse*.  The BOM
# is committed as part of the blob rather than generated on checkout, so every
# clone and every Git version behaves the same.  Python is unaffected (3.x
# treats source as UTF-8), and PowerShell 7 accepts both forms.
*.ps1   text eol=lf

# Never let Git touch these.
vendor/ffmpeg/* binary
assets/*        binary
*.png           binary
*.ico           binary
*.flac          binary
*.mflac         binary
*.qmcflac       binary
